{"comments":{"nodes":[{"author":{"login":"copilot-pull-request-reviewer"},"body":"This assertion depends on the host libc even though it overrides only OS and architecture. On an Alpine/musl host, the production detector correctly returns `linuxmusl-x64`, so this test fails while claiming the platform is Ubuntu. Derive the expected Linux classifier from the detected libc (or explicitly isolate the libc probe) to keep the unit suite portable while still asserting `linux-x64` on Ubuntu.","createdAt":"2026-07-30T20:12:03Z","databaseId":3685933779},{"author":{"login":"edburns"},"body":"Fixed in bc0275f8. The expected Linux classifier now derives from the host libc, preserving linux-x64 on glibc/Ubuntu and linuxmusl-x64 on musl.","createdAt":"2026-07-30T20:14:38Z","databaseId":3685949142}]},"id":"PRRT_kwDOQ5Wxys6VOywL","isResolved":true}
{"comments":{"nodes":[{"author":{"login":"copilot-pull-request-reviewer"},"body":"`phentsize` accepts any positive value, but the reads below assume a complete 56-byte ELF64 or 32-byte ELF32 program header. For example, a malformed probe with `phoff` at the last byte and `phentsize == 1` passes this check and then throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`; `detectLinuxLibc(Path)` only converts `IOException` to `UNKNOWN`, so this parse failure escapes the documented fallback. Validate the class-specific minimum entry size so malformed ELF data remains an `IOException`.","createdAt":"2026-07-30T20:12:04Z","databaseId":3685933797},{"author":{"login":"edburns"},"body":"Fixed in bc0275f8. ELF32 and ELF64 program headers now require their class-specific minimum entry sizes, with a malformed/truncated-header regression test confirming the Path-based detector falls back to UNKNOWN.","createdAt":"2026-07-30T20:14:40Z","databaseId":3685949304}]},"id":"PRRT_kwDOQ5Wxys6VOywW","isResolved":true}
